<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">State of Zhao</h1><p class="st-meta">403 BCE – 222 BCE · capital Handan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">One of the three successor states of Jin, famed for King Wuling's adoption of nomad cavalry and for its catastrophic defeat by Qin at Changping in 260 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Zhao lineage served as ministers of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-jin">State of Jin</a> for generations. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhao-xiangzi">Zhao Xiangzi</a> helped destroy the Zhi clan in 453 BCE, and in 403 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou</a> king recognised his successor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marquis-lie-of-zhao">Marquis Lie</a>, as a ruler. Zhao moved its capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/handan">Handan</a> in 386 BCE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Bordering the steppe, Zhao learned from its nomadic neighbours: in 307 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-wuling-of-zhao">King Wuling</a> ordered his troops to adopt trousers and mounted archery, then conquered <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zhongshan">Zhongshan</a>. Zhao produced famous ministers and generals, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lin-xiangru">Lin Xiangru</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lian-po">Lian Po</a> and Li Mu. But at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-changping">Changping</a> in 260 BCE the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/state-of-qin">State of Qin</a> destroyed its main army, reportedly killing hundreds of thousands of prisoners.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Zhao survived a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/handan">Handan</a> in 257 BCE, but Qin took the city in 228 BCE and captured King Qian. Prince Jia held out at Dai until 222 BCE. Zhao's horse-riding reforms influenced later Chinese warfare.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/state-of-zhao.jpg" alt="State of Zhao" width="320" height="295"><figcaption>Image: Philg88 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EN-ZHAO260BCE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>403 BCE – 222 BCE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Handan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Marquis Lie of Zhao, King Wuling of Zhao, King Qian of Zhao</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Zhou ancestral religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Zhao</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-312&amp;focus=state-of-zhao" aria-label="State of Zhao on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7556 858 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where State of Zhao lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7876 1114L7768 1187M7876 1114L8033 1602"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7768 1187h0M8033 1602h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7876 1114h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7876 1114h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Handan. <a href="/atlas?year=-312&amp;focus=state-of-zhao">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
